*.nbt filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text

# Approved reference imagery — the design gate's artifact — travels through LFS.
#
# A confirmed image is a CAMPAIGN FILE (the skill page's §4: "the moment images
# are confirmed they become campaign files"), so it has to be in the repository;
# an approval that lives only in a gitignored working directory is bound to
# nothing. But a 2K jpg is ~2.9 MB and does not delta-compress, so as a plain
# blob it lands in the object database at full size and stays there for every
# clone forever. Measured on one campaign's design set — 50 images, 138 MiB — a
# fresh single-branch clone went from a 4.5 MiB `.git` to a 145 MiB one. The
# owner's drill starts from a fresh clone, and two or three campaigns at that
# scale make the first command of the run a several-hundred-megabyte download.
#
# Through LFS the object database keeps a ~130-byte pointer per image instead,
# and the bytes are fetched for the revision you actually check out rather than
# for every revision that ever existed. Nothing new appears in the toolchain:
# `.nbt` already requires git-lfs, so the page's Init step 1 (`git lfs install`
# && `git lfs pull`) already covers these.
#
# Keyed on `design/`, not on `campaigns/`: the directory is what means "approved
# reference imagery", and a demo that grows one gets the same treatment without
# anybody remembering to widen a glob. Renders (`views/`, `review/`) and skins
# are deliberately NOT here — a skin is 4 KB and is read by tooling, and a
# render is regenerable from the piece it images.
#
# This changes what future commits store. Images already committed elsewhere
# stay exactly as they are: rewriting history to move them is a separate,
# destructive act nobody has asked for.
**/design/**/*.jpg filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
**/design/**/*.jpeg filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
**/design/**/*.png filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
**/design/**/*.webp filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
